experiment: f3name
  the question      §46 stage 1 / clause 3. The larynx COMMANDS f3 at
                    2.68 levels and the air CARRIES it at d' 5.272 once
                    rendered. Can it be TAUGHT? Asked before anything
                    ships, so dna/default.toml and the pinned hash are
                    untouched whatever the answer is.
  render_f3_gain    1.00  -- f3 reaches the ear
  the ask           f3 2613.7 -> 2741.1 Hz, half the measured reach, which is
                    ctxgrid's own convention for a target on an axis.
  the control       teachsound's YOKE: the same praise in the same
                    proportions, shifted half a trial so it cannot line
                    up with this creature's own utterances. The voice
                    drifts on its own, so the claim is never "f3 moved".

  CLAUSE 3a -- DID THE REWARD SEE F3? A zero error with a set target
  would mean the f3 field never reached the scorer, not that the lesson
  succeeded. `formant_error_f3` refuses an unset target rather than
  returning 0, so this is a wiring check with a firing branch.
    arm       scored   skipped   f3 err early f3 err late change    non-finite f1/f2/f3
    taught    393      35        0.04247      0.02272      +46.5     0/0/0
    yoked     393      35        0.04262      0.05009      -17.5     0/0/0
    the joint F1/F2 error the session reports, NOT this mode's objective:
      taught 0.1200 -> 0.0844    yoked 0.1300 -> 0.0748
    -> the scorer produced a graded f3 error in both windows

  CLAUSE 3b -- DID THE VOICE FOLLOW? f3 over the first and last thirds,
  against a target of 2741.1 Hz and a rest of 2613.7.
    taught    n 393    early   2634.3   late   2685.2   moved    +50.9 Hz
    yoked     n 393    early   2629.5   late   2608.9   moved    -20.5 Hz
    taught - yoked      +71.4 Hz   (the ask is +127.4)

  SINGLE SEED, SO THIS IS A GATE AND NOT A RESULT. It decides whether
  §46 clause 4 -- two words differing only in f3, indexed against a
  no-index control -- is worth building. A null here localises the
  refusal in the WRITE, with the axis already known audible (d' 5.272)
  and commandable (2.68 levels), and ends §46 at stage 1.
